window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"17-19","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"17-19-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-19-01/","title_he":"א – הזרוע הלחיים והקיבה","title_en":"The Foreleg, the Cheeks and the Maw","body_en":"<p>There is a Torah mitzva to give the kohanim three gifts from every domestic animal that is slaughtered: the foreleg (zero'a), the cheeks (leḥayayim) and the maw (keiva), as it is stated (Devarim 18:3): \"And this shall be the priests' due from the people, from those who slaughter a sacrifice, whether it be ox or sheep: they shall give to the priest the foreleg, and the two cheeks, and the maw.\" The reason for the mitzva is to help the kohanim with their livelihood so that they can study Torah and teach it to Israel.<sup>1</sup></p><p>The foreleg is the two upper joints of the animal's right front leg; the cheeks are the animal's two cheeks, including the tongue up to the beginning of the trachea; and the maw includes what is inside it and on it. In total, the gifts amount to about 8% of the weight of the kosher meat in the animal.</p><p>The mitzva is to give the gifts from kosher domestic animals, that is, from cattle, sheep and goats, and not from kinds of kosher wild animals such as gazelle and deer, nor from birds. If the animal was found to be a tereifa, the mitzva of the gifts is void (SA YD 61:6).</p><p>One must give the gifts to a kohen who is a Torah scholar, for the purpose of the gifts is to help the kohanim study Torah and teach it. When there is no kohen who is a Torah scholar there, one gives the gifts to a kohen who is an am ha-aretz (unlearned) (Ḥullin 130b, and Tosafot there; SA YD 61:7). And if the kohen who is a Torah scholar has enough for his needs, and the kohen who is not a Torah scholar is poor, such that there is a mitzva to give him tzedaka, it is permitted to give to the kohen who is an am ha-aretz (Tosafot Ḥullin 104b [?]; Yam Shel Shlomo there).</p><p>The mitzva is incumbent on Yisraelim to give their gifts to the kohanim, but a kohen who slaughtered his own animal is exempt from giving the gifts (SA YD 61:22). Likewise, a Yisrael married to a kohen's daughter is exempt from giving the gifts. Not only that, but one may give these gifts to a kohen's daughter who married a Yisrael or to her Yisrael husband, for as a result of the marriage the two are considered as one body. Even when there is a kohen there, one may give them the gifts (SA YD 61:8). And if the kohen's daughter is married to a Torah scholar, it is preferable to give them the gifts rather than to a kohen who is an am ha-aretz (Ḥullin 132a; Yam Shel Shlomo there; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 61:35).</p><p>The kohen is exempt from giving the gifts specifically when he slaughters for himself, but if the kohen earns his living by slaughtering animals in order to sell their meat, he is obligated to give the gifts to another kohen, and they would punish a kohen butcher who did not give his gifts to another kohen (Ḥullin 132b — according to Rashi the obligation is from the Torah, according to Tosafot from the words of the Sages; SA YD 61:24).</p><p>Regarding the Levi'im, the Sages were in doubt whether they are obligated to give the gifts, for the obligation is on 'the people', and perhaps this refers specifically to Yisraelim. Since the doubt concerns a monetary obligation, the rule is that \"the burden of proof is on the one who seeks to extract from his fellow,\" and since the kohanim cannot prove that the Levi'im are obligated to give them the gifts, they need not give (Ḥullin 131a; SA YD 61:23).</p><p>Some say that the mitzva is not dependent on the Land, and therefore one is obligated to fulfill it by Torah law in the Land and outside the Land, both when the Temple stands and when it is destroyed. And some say that it is dependent on the Land and its law is like that of terumot and ma'asrot, which one is obligated to fulfill in the Land and not outside the Land. This is the prevalent custom, and the meticulous give the gifts outside the Land as well. As to the force of the obligation of the mitzva, according to all the poskim, in all places under Israeli sovereignty the mitzva is from the Torah.<sup>2</sup></p><p>These gifts have no sanctity, and therefore the kohanim may eat them even in a state of impurity. The kohen may eat the gifts in any way he enjoys, and initially he should eat them in the manner most dignified for him. And if he has no preference, it is best that he eat them roasted with mustard, in the manner of kings (Ḥullin 132b; Tosafot s.v. 'ein'; SA YD 61:12). And if he does not want to eat the meat, he may give it away or sell it to a Jew or to a non-Jew (SA there 13).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Some say that one who gives the gifts recites the blessing \"al netinat matanot\" (\"concerning giving the gifts\") (Roke'aḥ 366; Damesek Eliezer). And some say that he also recites Sheheḥeyanu (Pe'at Ha-shulḥan 3:20, se'if katan 39). And some say that no blessing is recited, because by Torah law the gifts belong to the kohanim from the outset, and therefore setting them aside involves no new act of mitzva (Pri Ḥadash 61:1, based on Responsa Rashba 1:18 and Ramban; Knesset Ha-gedola; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 61:3-4). The halakha follows the majority of poskim that no blessing is recited."},{"n":2,"en":"According to the Rif, the Rambam (Hilkhot Bikkurim 9:1) and the Ḥinukh, the mitzva of the gifts applies by Torah law even after the destruction of the Temple and also outside the Land. This is also the plain meaning of the Gemara: since animals are not something that grows from the land, the mitzva is not dependent on the Land. On the other hand, according to many Rishonim, the halakha follows Rabbi Ila'i (Ḥullin 136a), that the law of the gifts is the same as that of terumot and ma'asrot, and just as one does not separate terumot and ma'asrot outside the Land, so one need not give the gifts outside the Land (Rashi, Tosafot, Ha-ma'or, Rashba and Ran). In practice, the prevalent custom is not to set aside the gifts from the slaughter of animals outside the Land (SA YD 61:21). Nevertheless, some of the great sages of Israel were meticulous in the mitzva and gave the gifts outside the Land, and so was the practice of the Ḥatam Sofer (YD 301). But in the Land it is agreed today that one must give the gifts by Torah law, as explained above 12:16, nn. 18-19."}]},{"slug":"17-19-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-19-02/","title_he":"ב – דרך נתינתן ודין בהמה שלא הורמו מתנותיה","title_en":"How They Are Given, and the Law of an Animal Whose Gifts Were Not Set Aside","body_en":"<p>The mitzva to give the gifts is incumbent on the owner of the animal, and if he gave the animal to a shoḥet to slaughter it for him, the responsibility to distribute the gifts to a kohen rests on the shoḥet. The shoḥet must ask the owner of the animal to which kohen he wishes to give the gifts (see SA YD 61:28).</p><p>It is a mitzva to hasten to give the gifts immediately after the sheḥita. The gifts must be given to the kohen in a respectful manner; therefore it is forbidden for a kohen to help the owner of the animal or the shoḥet in order that they give him the gifts. It is even forbidden for the kohen to ask for them, and all the more so it is forbidden for him to snatch them (Rambam, Hilkhot Bekhorot 1:14; SA YD 61:11). One should not divide the gifts into pieces that are too small, so as to give each kohen a significant gift (ibid. 9).</p><p>If there is no kohen there, they should assess the value of the gifts, and the owner of the animal may eat them or sell them, and afterwards give their monetary value to a kohen. This is so that the kohen will not lose his gifts, for if they did not eat the gifts, they would spoil and be lost (ibid. 10).</p><p>According to several Amora'im, it is forbidden to eat the meat of an animal whose gifts were not given to a kohen. However, according to halakha, the meat is permitted to eat, and it is a pious practice (midat ḥasidut) not to eat meat from an animal whose gifts were not given to a kohen (Megilla 28a; Ḥullin 37b; Tosafot there 131a s.v. 'ha\"g'; SA YD 61:5).</p><p>If the owner of an animal does not want to give the gifts to a kohen, if the beit din has the power, it should compel him to give the gifts to one of the kohanim (Taz 61:17). If the owner of the animal transgressed and ate the gifts, or sold them, or threw them in the trash, there is no way to obligate him to give their equivalent to a kohen, because there is no particular kohen who can claim their value. But according to the Torah he is obligated to repent and give their equivalent to a kohen (ibid. 15).</p><p>It is forbidden to buy or to receive as a gift a foreleg, cheeks and maw that were not given to a kohen, because one who buys or receives them becomes a partner in robbing the kohanim. After the fact, a Yisrael who bought or received the meat of gifts that were not given to a kohen, since the meat of the gifts is already with him, is permitted to eat it, for the responsibility to pay the value of the gifts to the kohen is on the owner of the animal (ibid. 31). However, as we have learned, it is a pious practice not to eat the meat of an animal whose gifts were not set aside, and all the more so one should not eat the gifts themselves that were stolen from the kohen.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-19-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-19-03/","title_he":"ג – מתן המתנות הלכה למעשה","title_en":"Giving the Gifts in Practice","body_en":"<p>In the past, when a private individual would slaughter an animal for the needs of his family, he would give the gifts to his neighbor the kohen. But today, when sheḥita is carried out in slaughterhouses where many animals are slaughtered every day, the quantity of gifts that accumulates in one day is enormous. If they were all brought to one kohen, or even to several kohanim, they would be unable to eat the meat of all the gifts even over a long time. And certainly it is not conceivable that the kohanim should open a business selling forelegs, cheeks and maws, for the purpose of the gifts is to help them study Torah and teach it, and not to turn them into merchants.</p><p>Therefore, the correct way is for the kohanim to receive the gifts and sell them to the owner of the business, and he will sell them to the consumers. However, in order for the gifts to come into the kohen's possession he would have to come to the slaughterhouse every day; to save this trouble, the owner of the business can give the kohen a loan at the beginning of every month, and they agree between them that the gifts designated for the kohen will be payment for the debt. In this way, even without the kohen actually receiving the gifts, they are considered his, because it was established in advance that they are designated for him. These kohanim are called 'makirei kehuna' (kohanim with a standing arrangement) (above 9:11). It is proper to choose as 'makirei kehuna' kohanim who are Torah scholars, who by virtue of the gifts will be strengthened in Torah study in order to teach it. The value of the gifts must be calculated according to what a wholesale meat merchant would be willing to pay for them, when the responsibility to kasher them of blood, package them and market them is on him. There are meat merchants who customarily pay the kohanim a paltry sum, knowing that the kohen has no way to claim his gifts. They bear the sin of wronging the kohanim and stealing their gifts.</p><p>And there are merchants who wish to evade the mitzva by entering into a partnership with a non-Jew, for an animal in which a non-Jew is a partner is exempt from the gifts. But if they established from the outset, as many do, that the front part will belong to the Jew and the hind part will belong to the non-Jew, because of the difficulty of removing the forbidden ḥelev and sinews from it, they are obligated to give the kohen the foreleg and the cheeks from the front part, since it belongs to the Jew (SA YD 61:25-27). Those who wish to be exempt from the mitzva must agree at the outset that the non-Jew is a partner in the entire animal, and only after the sheḥita should they divide the animal between them.</p><p>Unfortunately, most of the batei din tzedek (kashrut courts) and kashrut organizations do not supervise the fulfillment of this mitzva. Some evade the mitzva through a partnership with a non-Jew, and although they do not bear the sin of theft, this is an ugly way, to make a business of subterfuge in order to be exempt from fulfilling the mitzva. And some wrong the kohanim by paying a small amount for the gifts. In practice, the consumer may buy meat with any hekhsher, because the obligation to set aside the gifts is incumbent on the owners of the animals and the shoḥetim, whereas the buyers, as long as they do not know with certainty that the gifts were not set aside properly, may buy meat, and even a foreleg, cheeks and maw. However, it is a pious practice to verify that one is in fact buying meat from which the gifts were properly set aside, or that at least was exempt from setting aside the gifts because a non-Jew was a partner in it.</p><p>It is fitting to be strengthened in this mitzva and through it to support kohanim who are avrekhim and rabbis devoting their lives to Torah, to study it and teach it, in keeping with the aim of the mitzva. After all, every person must in any case give 'ma'aser kesafim' (a tithe of money) from his profits, and a fine standard is 'ḥomesh' (a fifth); so why should the meat merchants not set aside the gifts, and thereby fulfill both mitzvot at once?<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"The price of the priestly gifts to the customer in butcher shops is about 500-600 NIS per cow (5778 / 2018). Since these are expensive gifts, some look for ways to be exempt from the mitzva. Some say that since kohanim today have no proof of their lineage, one should not give them the gifts until they prove their lineage, for the burden of proof is on the one who seeks to extract from his fellow (see Mishneh Halakhot 13:108). And some say that since nowadays we do not adjudicate the lineage of kohanim, if the gifts are given on the basis of a person's testimony about himself, people may lie and claim that they are kohanim in order to receive the gifts (see Ḥazon Ish YD 7:3). However, in practice the Aḥaronim wrote that one should not evade the mitzva, and one is obligated to fulfill it in the Land of Israel (Knesset Ha-gedola, Pri Ḥadash, Ḥida, Rav Salant, Ḥevel Naḥalato 4:25).<br>The value of the gifts should be calculated according to what a wholesale meat merchant is willing to pay for such a quantity of meat, when the responsibility to kasher it, package it and market it is on him, which is less than half the price in the shops. Some cleverly calculate how much it would cost to transport the gifts from the slaughterhouse to the homes of the kohanim, where each kohen would be brought enough meat for his family's meal for one day, whose wholesale value is about 25 NIS, and since the kohen would have to pay for the transport, in effect there remains to give him a value of only a few shekels. And so instead of giving 250 NIS for the gifts of one animal according to their wholesale price, they give 20 NIS. But since it has been agreed to give the kohen the monetary value of the gifts, it can be deposited in his bank account at low cost, and consequently one must give him 250 NIS. It is true that one can pressure the kohen to agree to sell the gifts for a paltry price, for if he does not agree, they will give the gifts to another kohen who will agree to accept a paltry price, or they will make a partnership agreement with a non-Jew and be entirely exempt from the gifts. However, one who wishes to fulfill the mitzva properly must give the true value of the gifts.<br>The owners of the animals can of course count the gifts as <b>ma'aser kesafim</b>, for the main purpose of the enactment of ma'aser kesafim was to replace the obligation of terumot and ma'asrot and the giving of the gifts from animals. For when ninety percent of Israel earned their living from agriculture, everyone set aside close to twenty percent of the produce as terumot and ma'asrot. Likewise, from the animals they raised they set aside close to twenty percent: to the kohen they gave the foreleg, the cheeks and the maw, the first shearing and the firstborn, and they also set aside ma'aser behema (the animal tithe), similar to ma'aser sheni. Therefore, when many began to earn their living from commerce and industry, the Sages established that the average standard is to give a tenth, and a fine standard a fifth. When one gives the gifts to kohanim who are avrekhim, or to a yeshiva for the salary of a rabbi who is a kohen or a rabbi married to a kohen's daughter, what one gives fulfills the mitzva of ma'aser. The meat merchant should calculate his profits: if the value of the gifts comes to less than a tenth, he should add to tzedaka until he completes a tenth; and if it turns out that the cost of the gifts for him is more than a fifth of his profits, and it is difficult for him to give the full gifts because of competition with merchants who do not give the gifts, he should ask the kohen to treat him generously, and after he receives the gifts, to agree to accept for them a sum amounting to a fifth of the meat owner's profits, and in time of need, even a tenth alone."}]},{"slug":"17-19-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-19-04/","title_he":"ד – מצוות בכור בהמה וטעמה","title_en":"The Mitzva of the Firstborn Animal and Its Reason","body_en":"<p>Just as the Torah commanded to sanctify the firstborn son, so it commanded to sanctify the firstborn animal: every animal that gives birth to a male from its first pregnancy — it is holy, as it is stated (Shemot 13:2): \"Sanctify to Me every firstborn, whatever opens the womb among the Children of Israel, of man and of beast; it is Mine.\"</p><p>The sanctity of firstborns applies to the firstborns of kosher domestic animals and not to the firstborns of wild animals, for it is stated (Bamidbar 18:15): \"which they offer to the Lord\" — thus the mitzva of the firstborn applies to a species from which a sacrifice is offered to the Lord, and not to species of wild animals from which no sacrifice is offered. The species of domestic animals from which sacrifices are offered are three: cattle, sheep and goats.</p><p>The firstborn animal is one of the gifts that the Torah commanded Israel to give to the kohanim, so that they could fulfill their spiritual role. Generally the firstborn was unblemished, and then the kohen had a mitzva to offer it as a sacrifice and to eat of its meat in purity in Jerusalem with his household. And if a blemish befell it, the kohen slaughters it anywhere and eats it with his household like ordinary (ḥullin) meat. This mitzva applies to all animals belonging to Jews, both in the Land and outside the Land (SA YD 306:1).<sup>4</sup></p><p>Sanctifying the firstborn educates a person to humility and fear of Heaven, for after his animal's first birth, he might become proud and think that he is the one who manages his life and the lives of his animals, and forget that God gives life to all. Just as the Egyptians denied God and thought that all their wealth and power came from themselves, and as a result denied the foundations of morality and cruelly enslaved Israel, and in the end they and their animals were punished with the plague of the firstborn, to make known to the world that even the lives of the most honored of men depend on God, and if they act wickedly — they will be punished. So that we always remember this foundation, we were commanded to sanctify the firstborn of man and beast to God. If the opening of the womb was through the birth of a female, there is no mitzva to sanctify it, because in a male the trait of pride is liable to stand out more, and to rectify it one must sanctify it and give it to the kohen. In addition, the aim of the mitzva is to give the kohanim a gift according to the proportion of firstborn males, and if one also had to sanctify firstborn females, the measure would be double the aim of the mitzva.</p><p>The mitzva of firstborns also reveals the holiness hidden in reality. The Holy One, blessed be He, after all, created and gives life to everything, and consequently there is holiness in all creatures, but it is concealed and hidden; and through the mitzva to sanctify the firstborn animal and give it to the kohen, the holiness hidden in animality is revealed. It is revealed specifically in the firstborn, because it opens its mother's womb, and in it the primary, primordial foundation that begins the life cycle of the new generation is revealed. So that it expresses faith and not pride, we were commanded to sanctify it and give it to the kohen. As a result, the other animals that will be born will also continue the direction of holiness and help Israel reveal the word of God in the routine of ordinary life.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"The law of the firstborn and of the animal tithe is binding outside the Land as well, since they are mitzvot not dependent on the Land, for the animal does not grow from the land. But one may not bring them as sacrifices, since they are compared to ma'aser sheni, which does not apply outside the Land. Rather, if they are unblemished — they graze until they become blemished, and if they are blemished — they are eaten, the firstborn by the kohen and the animal tithe by its owner. And if despite this they brought the firstborn or the animal tithe to the Temple, according to Rabbi Akiva — they do not offer them, and according to Rabbi Yishmael — they offer them (Temura 21a-b; Bekhorot 53a). The halakha follows Rabbi Akiva (Rambam, Hilkhot Bekhorot 1:5; 6:2; Ra'avad, Ramban, Rosh, Rashba, Semag, and others).<br>There was, however, an erroneous version of the Rambam according to which the law of the firstborn does not apply outside the Land, and so wrote the Me'iri. The Ḥinukh wrote that the obligation of firstborns outside the Land is rabbinic. However, the other poskim wrote that their words are based on an erroneous version. In practice, since in fact there is no possibility of offering a firstborn from outside the Land, it is preferable to be exempted from the obligation through a partnership with a non-Jew. It is possible that this was the practice outside the Land when the Temple stood, and consequently it was accepted in Israel that the mitzva of the firstborn animal is not observed outside the Land — not because there is no obligation, but because they were accustomed to find a way to be exempt from it, since there was no possibility of fulfilling it as commanded."}]},{"slug":"17-19-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-19-05/","title_he":"ה – פרטי דיני בכור בהמה","title_en":"Details of the Laws of the Firstborn Animal","body_en":"<p>There are two aspects to the mitzva of the firstborn animal: one, that it has sanctity and must be offered to God; the second, that it is a gift among the twenty-four gifts that the Torah commanded to give to the kohanim so that they would be free for the service of God.</p><p>Since the firstborn has sanctity, every firstborn animal becomes sanctified, whether it belongs to a kohen, a Levi or a Yisrael; but if the animal of a Yisrael or a Levi gave birth to a firstborn — it must be given to a kohen. And if the animal of a kohen gave birth to a firstborn, the kohen must separate it from his other animals and offer it on the altar, and he and his household eat of its meat, like the law of a firstborn he received from a Yisrael.</p><p>Since the firstborn is intended to be a gift to the kohen, even when a blemish befell the firstborn and it is not fit for a sacrifice, it is a mitzva to give it to the kohen, and the members of the kohen's family are permitted to eat it anywhere, even when they are impure. Likewise, the kohen may sell its meat to a Yisrael, on condition that he does not sell it in a butcher shop, for it is not respectful to the mitzva that the meat of the firstborn be sold in a store (SA YD 306:6).</p><p>One does not give the firstborn animal to the kohen when it is very small, so as not to trouble the kohen with caring for it. Rather, the giver must keep it with him until it grows a little, and afterwards give it to the kohen: for a sheep or a goat until it is thirty days old, and for an ox until it is fifty days old. And if after that time he did not find a kohen, he must care for it until he finds a kohen (SA YD 306:2). In any case, one must give the firstborn to the kohen within its first year, so that the kohen can offer it on the altar before it is a year old, as it is stated (Devarim 15:20): \"You shall eat it before the Lord your God year by year.\" Even a firstborn that became blemished must be given to the kohen within its first year (SA YD 306:7).</p><p>Since the firstborn is holy, it is forbidden to make use of it, whether it is unblemished or blemished. For example, it is forbidden to plow the field with a firstborn ox, and likewise it is forbidden to shear the wool of a firstborn sheep, and if one transgressed and sheared it — the wool is forbidden for benefit. But if a blemish befell the firstborn sheep and it was slaughtered for eating, the wool on its hide is permitted for benefit (SA YD 308:1).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-19-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-19-06/","title_he":"ו – בעיית הטיפול בבכור בזמן הזה","title_en":"The Problem of Caring for the Firstborn Nowadays","body_en":"<p>Nowadays, when the Temple is destroyed, there is no possibility of offering an unblemished firstborn. Therefore, when a firstborn is born, one must wait until a blemish befalls it that disqualifies it from being a sacrifice, and only then may the kohen slaughter it and eat its meat. And although caring for the unblemished firstborn involves enormous trouble, it is forbidden for the kohen to refuse to accept it, because if he refuses, he will appear to be disparaging the priestly gifts. Likewise, it is forbidden for the kohen to put it in an enclosed place in order to kill it by starvation, because he thereby causes the loss of consecrated property. Likewise, it is forbidden for the kohen to cause a blemish to befall it; for example, it is forbidden to stick dough on the firstborn's ear so that a dog will come and bite the dough together with the ear and inflict a blemish on it. Likewise, it is forbidden to ask a non-Jew to inflict a blemish on it. Rather, the kohen must accept it and care for it, and if a blemish befalls it on its own — he may slaughter it, and if no blemish befalls it — he must care for it until its death, and then bury it, for its meat and hide are forbidden for benefit (SA YD 313:1-3; 309:1).</p><p>In order to avoid the problem of caring for firstborns, the practice is to sell the animals that are about to give birth for the first time (mavkirot) to a non-Jew, as will be explained in the next section. If the Yisrael did not sell his animal with the intention that it give birth to a holy firstborn and that it be given to the kohen in order to cause him distress thereby — the kohen is not obligated to accept the firstborn from him (Rema 306:4). And if the Yisrael was negligent in that he neglected to sell the mavkirot to a non-Jew, but did not intend to provoke the kohen by this, a doubt arose whether the kohen is obligated to accept the firstborn from him and care for it, or whether the kohen can say that since the Yisrael was negligent, it is the Yisrael who must care for the firstborn until it dies or a blemish befalls it (Ḥatam Sofer YD 302; Pitḥei Teshuva YD 306:2-3).</p><p>As we have learned, if the firstborn was born inadvertently, the kohen is obligated to accept it and care for it until the day of its death. Since this is so, the kohanim were very interested in a blemish befalling it, to the point that the Sages said that the kohanim are suspected of inflicting blemishes on firstborns. Therefore, if a blemish befell the firstborn that could have been caused by a person, the kohen must bring a witness to testify that the blemish occurred on its own, and if he has no witness, the firstborn is not rendered fit for sheḥita on the basis of that blemish (SA YD 314:1).</p><p>And if there was a witness who saw that the blemish occurred on its own — they show the blemish to an expert versed in the laws of blemishes. When there is no expert, they show it to three Torah scholars, one of whom is familiar with these laws, and if they ruled that a permanent blemish befell it, one that will not heal — it is permitted to slaughter it (SA YD 309:2). And if they slaughtered it without showing it to a sage — its meat is forbidden to eat. And even if after the sheḥita a sage saw that a permanent blemish had indeed befallen it, since he did not show it to a sage first, the Sages penalized him that its meat is forbidden to eat. Some were lenient with obvious blemishes (SA YD 310:1; Taz 1).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-19-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-19-07/","title_he":"ז – שותפות עם נוכרי פוטרת את הבכור מקדושה","title_en":"Partnership with a Non-Jew Exempts the Firstborn from Sanctity","body_en":"<p>The firstborn animal becomes sanctified when it is born to an animal belonging to a Jew; and even if it belongs to partners, if all the partners are Jews — the firstborn is holy. But if the animal belongs to a non-Jew, or a non-Jew has a partnership in it, there is no sanctity in the firstborn it bears.</p><p>Nowadays, when the Temple is destroyed, one should make a partnership with a non-Jew in the mavkirot animals in order to be exempt from the law of firstborns. This is because the mitzva is to offer the firstborns as sacrifices, and since the Temple is destroyed we cannot offer them; yet it is forbidden to slaughter them or work with them, and one must care for them until a blemish befalls them, and only then will the kohen be permitted to slaughter them and eat their meat. In practice, most animals will not become blemished until the day of their death, and the kohanim will have to care for them for many years without return. And since there will be kohanim who will not withstand this, and will deliberately inflict a blemish on the firstborns, or make use of them despite their sanctity, the Rishonim ruled to make a non-Jew a partner in the mavkirot, so that there will be no sanctity in the firstborns. And although by this sale one cancels the mitzva, it is better to be exempt from the mitzva than to enter the risk of the mishap of deriving benefit from a firstborn (Tosafot Bekhorot 3b; SA YD 320:6).</p><p>For the non-Jew to be a partner, one must sell him part of the animal, and one must sell such a limb that if it were removed — the animal would die or be considered blemished. For example, one can sell the non-Jew the esophagus of all the mavkirot, or their ears, and thereby any firstborn born from them will not be holy (SA YD 320:4).</p><p>One must be precise that the sale is done through an act of acquisition (kinyan) that is effective between a Jew and a non-Jew according to Torah law; and since the Rishonim disagreed about this, one should sell to him according to both views: with money, according to the opinion of Rashi, and by meshikha (pulling), according to the opinion of Rabbeinu Tam. That is, the non-Jew should give the Jew a peruta for his share in the animals. And although those limbs are worth more than a peruta, since the Jew wishes to sell him those limbs for a peruta in order to be exempt from the law of firstborns — his sale is valid. In addition, he should transfer the animals to him through the kinyan of meshikha, by the non-Jew pulling the mavkirot into his domain. And if there is no land there belonging to him, the Jew should rent to the non-Jew the place on which the mavkirot stand, and thereby the non-Jew acquires his share in the mavkirot (SA YD 320:6).</p><p>The poskim disagreed whether one can sell the non-Jew his share through the kinyan accepted by the law of that country. According to most poskim, since 'dina de-malkhuta dina' (the law of the land is the law), a kinyan made according to the law of the land is effective, and the non-Jew becomes a partner in the animals, and there is no sanctity in the firstborns that will be born to them (Knesset Yeḥezkel 14; Ḥatam Sofer YD 314). And some say that only a kinyan according to the Torah is effective (Divrei Ḥayim 2:117). Initially one should take their view into account and sell the non-Jew his share in the mavkirot also through the kinyanim effective according to the Torah.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-19-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-19-08/","title_he":"ח – פטר חמור","title_en":"Peter Ḥamor (The Firstborn Donkey)","body_en":"<p>After the Torah commanded concerning the sanctity of human firstborns and the firstborns of kosher animals, it further commanded concerning peter ḥamor, which is the firstborn donkey that opens its mother's womb, as it is stated (Shemot 13:13): \"And every firstling of a donkey you shall redeem with a lamb, and if you do not redeem it, you shall break its neck.\" Although the firstborn donkey has sanctity, since it is a non-kosher species it cannot be offered on the altar; therefore the Torah commanded to redeem it with a lamb (seh), whereupon the sanctity of the firstborn donkey lapses, and it is permitted to use it for ordinary purposes. The lamb itself also does not become holy; rather, it must be given to the kohen, and the kohen may do with it whatever he wishes. This too is one of the twenty-four priestly gifts intended to sustain the kohanim so that they can be free to teach Torah in Israel.</p><p>Since the peter ḥamor has sanctity, as long as it has not been redeemed it is forbidden to work with it, and it is forbidden to derive benefit from its hair. If its hair became tangled and bothers the donkey, it is permitted to cut it, but it is forbidden to derive benefit from it (SA YD 321:9).</p><p>One can redeem the donkey with anything equal in value to the donkey: if the peter ḥamor is worth ten dinars — one can redeem it with anything worth ten dinars. The advantage of redemption with a lamb is that even if the lamb is worth only one dinar, one can redeem any donkey with it, even if it is worth ten dinars (ibid. 5).</p><p>Since there is a mitzva in redeeming the peter ḥamor, the Sages instituted a blessing for it. The practice is first to lift the lamb for the purpose of setting it aside, and to recite: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who has sanctified us with His commandments and commanded us concerning the redemption of the firstling of a donkey.\" And afterwards one gives the lamb to the kohen.</p><p>When the owner of the donkey refuses to redeem it, since the donkey is holy and it is forbidden to use it, he must break its neck, as it is stated (Shemot 13:13): \"And if you do not redeem it, you shall break its neck.\" The neck-breaking is done with a large knife from the back of the neck. But initially one should redeem the donkey with a lamb and give the lamb to the kohen, because in this way one gives the gift to the kohen; moreover, it is not fitting to kill the donkey for nothing (SA YD 321:12; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 321:2).</p><p>Generally, the sanctity of firstborns is revealed in species that can reveal holiness: in man, who can sanctify his life, and in domestic animals, which can be offered as sacrifices. But in species of kosher wild animals that are not offered on the altar, such as deer and gazelle, there is no sanctity of firstborns, and all the more so there is no sanctity of firstborns in species of non-kosher domestic and wild animals. Only the firstborns of donkeys did the Torah exclude from the rule and establish that they have sanctity, to teach us that even in the non-kosher species of animals holiness is hidden, but this holiness is very concealed, and is revealed only in the peter ḥamor — though not as a sacrifice, but in such a way that it must be redeemed and its equivalent given to the kohen.</p><p>From the sanctity of the peter ḥamor we learn that in essence the whole purpose of the non-kosher animals is to be partners in repairing the world through the word of God. And although it is a Divine decree that the holiness in the non-kosher species should be revealed specifically through donkeys, there is a reason for it: the donkeys helped the Children of Israel, for on them they loaded the great wealth when they left Egypt (Bekhorot 5b). And this is fitting, for the donkey (ḥamor) alludes to simple materiality (ḥomriyut), which at a superficial glance seems cut off from the Divine destiny; and through the holiness in the peter ḥamor it becomes clear that its destiny is to serve as a basis for revealing holiness, just as the donkeys helped Israel carry the material wealth that served as a basis for the service of God and the building of the Mishkan. And so the Sages alluded (Sanhedrin 98a) that the Mashiaḥ will ride on a donkey; that is, he will draw matter near and make it a vehicle for the revelation of the Shekhina, in order to reveal the Divine ideals in the world.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-19-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-19-09/","title_he":"ט – מעשר בהמה","title_en":"Ma'aser Behema (The Animal Tithe)","body_en":"<p>Just as we were commanded to set aside 'ma'aser sheni' (the second tithe) from produce in years 1, 2, 4 and 5 of the shemita cycle and to eat it in sanctity in Jerusalem, so we were commanded to set aside in all years a tithe from animals, to bring it up to Jerusalem, offer it on the altar and eat its meat in sanctity, as it is stated (Vayikra 27:32): \"And all the tithe of the herd or of the flock, whatever passes under the rod, the tenth shall be holy to the Lord.\"</p><p>What is special about 'ma'aser behema' and the produce of 'ma'aser sheni' is that they remain in the possession of their owners, who must bring them up to Jerusalem and eat them in sanctity and purity. The animal set aside as a tithe was thereby consecrated as a sacrifice; therefore they would sprinkle its blood and burn its fats on the altar, and the meat was eaten by the owner of the animal and his relatives (Rambam, Hilkhot Bekhorot 6:4).</p><p>Through the mitzva of 'ma'aser behema' we uncover another facet of the holiness hidden in animals: not only is the firstborn sanctified and given to the kohen, but in addition a tenth of the animals is designated for a sacrifice that will draw all of Israel close to God and to the sacred values revealed in Jerusalem. For the mitzva is to eat the meat of the sacrifice within the walls of the city of Jerusalem, and during their stay in Jerusalem Israel would be suffused with its holiness and would study Torah from the kohanim and from the great sages who resided there. A person who had many animals as tithe would, in order to finish eating their meat, invite the poor and Levi'im to his meal, and thereby fulfill an additional mitzva. He could also encourage his son or one of his relatives to go up to Jerusalem and study Torah there, and meanwhile eat of the meat of 'ma'aser behema' and the produce of 'ma'aser sheni', and through this the number of Torah learners in Israel would increase (above 7:10).</p><p>The obligation to set aside 'ma'aser behema' applies to every Jew, whether kohen or Yisrael, whether in the Land or outside the Land. It applies to all the lambs, males and females, unblemished and blemished, that were born in his flock during one year, from 1 Tishrei until 29 Elul. But one whose animals bore only nine lambs in a year is exempt from 'ma'aser behema'. The obligation to set aside applies to the three kinds of domestic animals offered as sacrifices: cattle, sheep and goats. However, the small domestic animals, sheep and goats, combine for the count of ten, whereas the offspring of large domestic animals, cows, are counted separately.</p><p>This is the order of setting aside: one brings the lambs into a pen and opens a small opening for them, and stands their mothers outside, and they bleat and call to them, and the lambs go out, and he counts them one by one; and when the tenth comes to go out, whether male or female, whether unblemished or blemished, he marks it with red paint, and it is consecrated. If he had nineteen lambs — he consecrates one; if he had twenty — he consecrates two. If he had ninety-nine — he consecrates nine; if he had a hundred — he consecrates ten.</p><p>If the tenth that came out was blemished, it has no sanctity, and there is no need to bring it up to Jerusalem; rather, it is permitted to slaughter it and eat its meat anywhere, but it is forbidden to use it for work, and likewise it is forbidden to shear its wool while it is alive in order to benefit from it. Unlike the firstborn, which requires testimony that the blemish occurred on its own, because we are concerned that the kohen may have inflicted a blemish on it in order to permit it, with ma'aser behema no testimony is needed; rather, we rely on the owner of the animal. For if he had wanted to be exempt from ma'aser behema, he could have inflicted a blemish on his entire flock and then tithed it, and consequently the tithe would be blemished and have no sanctity (Rambam, Hilkhot Bekhorot 6:9).</p><p>Unlike the law of produce, which is forbidden to eat before terumot and ma'asrot have been set aside from it, the lambs may be slaughtered or sold before the end of the year, and if he sells or slaughters until he does not have ten left — he will be exempt from setting aside ma'aser behema. But the Sages instituted that at three times a year they should set aside ma'aser behema, fifteen days before each of the three festivals: Pesaḥ, Shavu'ot and Sukkot. From the time of setting aside ma'aser behema it is forbidden for a person to sell his lambs or slaughter them without setting aside ma'aser behema from them. They instituted these times so that animals would be available to the pilgrims for sacrifices and for eating in Jerusalem (Rambam, Hilkhot Bekhorot 7:8).</p><p>After the destruction of the Temple, since it is not possible to offer 'ma'aser behema', the Sages established that ma'aser behema should not be set aside, because of fear of a mishap, lest they slaughter an unblemished animal that was set aside as a tithe, and violate the karet prohibition of slaughtering consecrated animals outside [the Temple]. There is also a concern that they will use the animal set aside as a tithe for work and shearing (Bekhorot 53a). If one transgressed and set aside ma'aser behema nowadays, it should be left to die, so that they will not come to a mishap (Bekhorot 61a). According to the Rambam one may wait, perhaps a blemish will befall it and it will be permitted to slaughter it for eating (Hilkhot Bekhorot 6:2).<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"The Arukh Ha-shulḥan He-atid (Kodashim 201:13-14) explained that the Sages are entitled to annul a mitzva passively (shev ve-al ta'aseh), as explained in Yevamot 90a. Moreover, it is impossible to fulfill this mitzva nowadays, for it is stated: \"shall be holy,\" that is, for offering, and now there is no possibility of offering. In the Gemara they asked: why did they not annul firstborns? And they explained that the firstborn is holy from its mother's womb and it is not in the power of the Sages to annul it, whereas with ma'aser behema they annulled the process that sanctifies the tenth animal. The Arukh Ha-shulḥan added that although they instituted to sell the mavkira animal, this is not an obligation, so as not to make it obligatory to cancel the sanctity of the firstborn.<br>According to Rabbeinu Ḥananel, a kohen is exempt from ma'aser behema, and according to the other poskim he is obligated. The view accepted by the vast majority of poskim is that the law of 'ma'aser behema' is like the law of 'ma'aser sheni', which is intended to be eaten by the owners in Jerusalem (Rambam, Hilkhot Bekhorot 6:4). However, some say that 'ma'aser behema' is given to the kohen as part of the priestly gifts (Ritva Shabbat 54b; see Rav Y. Perla, positive commandments 86-88).<br>Regarding the counting of tenths: animals left over beyond the count of tenths before one of the festivals combine with the next count of tenths until the end of the year, and only animals left over at the end of the year outside the count of tenths are exempt from the tithe and do not combine with the count of the following year (Bekhorot 57b)."}]},{"slug":"17-19-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-19-10/","title_he":"י – ראשית הגז","title_en":"Reishit Ha-gez (The First Shearing)","body_en":"<p>Reishit ha-gez is one of the twenty-four gifts that it is a mitzva to give to the kohanim so that they will have wool for spinning their garments, as it is stated (Devarim 18:4-5): \"The first fruits of your grain, of your wine and of your oil, and the first of the fleece of your sheep, you shall give him. For the Lord your God has chosen him out of all your tribes, to stand to minister in the name of the Lord, him and his sons forever.\"</p><p>By Torah law there is no fixed measure for giving the fleece, and even if one gave any amount to the kohen — he has fulfilled his obligation. But the Sages set a measure: some say one-sixtieth of the shearing (Rambam, Hilkhot Bikkurim 10:1), and some say the weight of five sela'im, which is about 100 grams of wool, from which a belt (avnet) can be made (Tur YD 333:13). There is no sanctity in reishit ha-gez, and therefore the kohen may do with it whatever he wishes.</p><p>Yisraelim and Levi'im, men and women, are obligated in reishit ha-gez. But a Yisrael married to a kohen's daughter is exempt from reishit ha-gez, and since there is no sanctity in reishit ha-gez, it is permitted to give it also to a kohen's daughter married to a Yisrael (SA YD 333:14, and as explained in section 1).</p><p>The Tanna'im disagreed about whether the mitzva of reishit ha-gez applies outside the Land as well, and the halakhic ruling is that it does not apply outside the Land, just as terumot and ma'asrot do not apply outside the Land (Ḥullin 136b; SA YD 333:1). Regarding the force of the mitzva of 'reishit ha-gez' in the Land of Israel, according to most poskim it is from the Torah, and some say its obligation is rabbinic, like the law of terumot and ma'asrot (above 12:16, n. 18).</p><p>The mitzva is to give reishit ha-gez from sheep, both males and females, because it is from them that wool is usually prepared, but not from goats or other species. If a sheep's wool is so coarse that it is not fit for clothing, it is exempt from reishit ha-gez (SA YD 333:2).</p><p>The Sages learned that only one who has at least five sheep, and the weight of the wool of each of them is at least 12 sela'im — about 204 grams — is obligated in reishit ha-gez. The weight of the wool shorn from an adult sheep of the Awassi breed common in the Land is about two and a half kilograms, so that there is almost no adult sheep whose wool weighs less than 204 grams, and naturally from every five sheep one must set aside reishit ha-gez (SA YD 333:9).</p><p>These five sheep need not be shorn in one year; rather, even if he shore one sheep each year, if at the time he shore the first sheep all five sheep were in his ownership, he will need to set aside 'reishit ha-gez' from the fifth (SA YD 333:12).</p><p>If the sheep belong to several partners, the obligation of reishit ha-gez applies only when each of the partners has the measure of five sheep.</p><p>If he had several kinds of sheep, he should set aside wool for the kohen from the finest kind, since it is stated (Devarim 18:4-5): \"the first of the fleece of your sheep\" (Rema 333:12). Initially he should set aside from the shearing of the first sheep, but only after he finishes shearing the five sheep should he give the gift to the kohen, for only then does he become obligated in 'reishit ha-gez' ('Gez Tzonkha' 333:11:1).</p><p>Initially one gives the kohen wool in its raw state, that is, before it is cleaned of the fats and dirt that adhered to it. And if he has already cleaned the wool, he should set aside from the clean wool and give it to the kohen (ibid. 13:5).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-19-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-19-11/","title_he":"יא – קיום המצווה בימינו","title_en":"Fulfilling the Mitzva Today","body_en":"<p>Today, in the Land of Israel, there is no natural opportunity to fulfill the mitzva, because the wool of the sheep of the Land of Israel is simple and coarse, and only thick threads are made from it, which are of no use to the kohen. Moreover, with this kind of wool, the price of shearing is higher than the price of the wool, and the shearing is done only for the sake of the sheep, so that their wool will not weigh on them. Therefore, nowadays the practice is to pay money to the shearers and in addition to give them the wool as their wages. In such a situation, it is also not worthwhile for the kohen to deal with the wool he would receive. Nevertheless, one should not argue that this wool is not fit for clothing and is exempt from the mitzva of 'reishit ha-gez', for in truth it is fit to be worn; rather, following the rise in the standard of living, finer wool is preferred today for the clothing industry.</p><p>If, in return for the shearers' agreement to shear, the owner of the sheep sold all the wool of the sheep to Jewish shearers, the mitzva rests on the shearers (Rambam). And some say that if all the wool was sold in return for the shearing while it was still attached to the sheep, the wool is exempt from the mitzva of 'reishit ha-gez', for at the time of shearing the wool no longer belongs to the owner of the sheep, and the shearers are exempt because they are not the owners of the sheep (Rema YD 333:8, as explained by the Shakh 8; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 333:8).</p><p>An additional problem exists regarding the ownership of the sheep. For we have learned (in section 7) that in order to avoid the problem of firstborns, the practice is to sell the esophagus of the mavkirot to a non-Jew, and thereby he becomes a partner in the mavkira animal, and the firstborn born to it is not holy. And with regard to 'reishit ha-gez', any animal in which a non-Jew is a partner does not count toward the five sheep from whose wool one must set aside 'reishit ha-gez'.</p><p>Therefore, one who wishes to fulfill the mitzva of 'reishit ha-gez' today must first ensure that the sheep are in his exclusive ownership, and shear them himself or through an agent, and set aside from the shearing a measure of two hundred grams, so that after cleaning the weight of the wool will remain at least one hundred grams, and give the fleece to the kohen. And although it is likely that the kohen will not use the fleece, nevertheless the one who sets it aside has fulfilled the mitzva with it.</p><p>Some say that no blessing is recited over 'reishit ha-gez', because the Yisrael does not give of his own; rather, God, blessed be He, granted the gifts to the kohanim (Rabbi Yitzḥak ben Palti [?], Ramban). And some say that one recites the blessing \"al netinat reishit ha-gez\" (\"concerning giving the first shearing\"), because 'reishit ha-gez' is not like the foreleg, cheeks and maw, which belong to the kohen from the outset; rather, reishit ha-gez must be designated at the time of shearing, and therefore one who sets it aside fulfills a real mitzva (Roke'aḥ, Pe'at Ha-shulḥan). And so ruled in practice our teacher and rabbi, Rav Tzvi Yehuda Ha-kohen Kook, of blessed memory.</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
